{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-333576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a first-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3}$\n- $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{5}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-333576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}=6(Δx)^{2+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{2}=0.3(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $9(Δx)^{3}=9(Δx)^{2+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-333576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b40a29b201074595f15b3097b470884390ec1df26122bbda5a4bb5a525ac716c"} {"question" "q-333576" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-333576" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-333576" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.35,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.35)=0.75.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.10,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.10)=0.005.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.35$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.35}{0.10}=3.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(3.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.75$ to $0.005.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.75}{0.005}=150.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3.5)^{p+1} & ≈150 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3.5}⁡(150) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.999… \\\\ p & ≈2.999….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b40a29b201074595f15b3097b470884390ec1df26122bbda5a4bb5a525ac716c"} {"question" "q-334648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}-(Δx)^{6}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.4,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.6) & =6(0.6)^{5}-(0.6)^{6} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.077\\,76-0.046\\,656 \\\\  & =0.466\\,56-0.046\\,656 \\\\  & =0.419\\,904 \\\\  & ≈0.420.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.4) & =6(0.4)^{5}-(0.4)^{6} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.010\\,24-0.004\\,096 \\\\  & =0.061\\,44-0.004\\,096 \\\\  & =0.057\\,344 \\\\  & ≈0.057.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.4.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.4}=1.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={1.5}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.419\\,904$ to $0.057\\,344.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.419\\,904}{0.057\\,344}=7.322\\,54…≈7.323.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1.5}^{p+1} & ≈7.322\\,54… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{1.5}⁡(7.322\\,54…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.910… \\\\ p & ≈3.910….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}-(Δx)^{6}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{5})=O\\,((Δx)^{4+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b40a29b201074595f15b3097b470884390ec1df26122bbda5a4bb5a525ac716c"} {"question" "q-334710" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334710" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.057"] ["math" "0.075"] ["math" "0.375"] ["math" "0.420"] ["math" "0.550"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334710" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.420"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334710" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334710" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.057"] ["math" "0.075"] ["math" "0.225"] ["math" "0.420"] ["math" "0.550"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334710" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.057"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334710" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334710" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10.065"] ["math" "4.361"] ["math" "5.957"] ["math" "7.323"] ["math" "8.603"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334710" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7.323"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334710" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334710" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334710" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{4}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{7}$\n- $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{5}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let’s check each option:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}=6(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.5(Δx)^{4}=0.5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{2}=2(Δx)^{2}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b40a29b201074595f15b3097b470884390ec1df26122bbda5a4bb5a525ac716c"} {"question" "q-334761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $3.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.2$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.2)≈0.064.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.2$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.2}{0.1}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $3,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{3}={2}^{3}=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.1)≈\\frac{0.064}{8}=0.008.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b40a29b201074595f15b3097b470884390ec1df26122bbda5a4bb5a525ac716c"} {"question" "q-334763" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"} {"question" "q-334763" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.008"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060090/state.json" "file_sha256" "b17bb17c30e3da2c4a630ae156be6c9d2b0f8c4ba12dd5a651845f9094726aa2"}] "usage" {"q-333576" []} "basis" 1915 "retractions" [[17592186221911 :question/problem "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a first-order numerical method?\n\n1. $τ(Δx) = 6(Δx)^{3}$\n2. $τ(Δx) = 0.3(Δx)^{2} + 2(Δx)^{3}$\n3. $τ(Δx) = 9(Δx)^{3} + 4(Δx)^{5}$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186220418 17592186220419 17592186220420 17592186220421 17592186220422] "immutable_fields" [[17592186220417 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220417 :answer-field/id] [17592186220417 :answer-field/key] [17592186220417 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
